# Flash from a dataset

[Back to PlussPl by example](../guide.md)

Many `flash` functions take a whole `Dataset` and the names of its columns, so the program says which variables to plot instead of extracting them. This page lists a bundled dataset, draws joint, pair and triplots of it by name, and then does the same for your own table and your own matrix.

## How it works

`flash::load_dataset(name)` returns one of thirteen bundled datasets. A `Dataset` keeps numeric, text and temporal columns separately, with `numeric_names`, `text_names` and `temporal_names` listing them, and reports `nrows()` and `ncols()`. `jointplot(studio, data, x_name, y_name, kind)` plots two named columns, with `kind` one of `"scatter"`, `"reg"`, `"hex"` or `"kde"`, or a `JointPlotOptions` struct for finer control. `pairplot(studio, data, PairPlotOptions)` and `triplot(studio, data, TriplotOptions)` use every numeric column, restricted by the options. Your own data enters through `Dataset::from_table(frame)` for a `DataFrame`, or through a `MatrixFrameOptions` that names the feature axis and the features of a matrix.

## Exploring a dataset

The program prints the shape and columns of `petals`, displays every joint kind, every pair-grid triangle and every triplot kind, and wraps a table and a matrix of its own.

@@example flash/dataset_by_name.cpp@@

Four kinds of joint view of the same two columns:

![scatter](assets/flash_joint_scatter.svg)

![reg](assets/flash_joint_reg.svg)

![hex](assets/flash_joint_hex.svg)

![kde](assets/flash_joint_kde.svg)

